0,Tequistlatecan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tequistlatecan_languages,"The Tequistlatecan languages, also called Chontal, are three close but distinct languages spoken or once spoken by the Chontal people of Oaxaca State, Mexico.Chontal. Geographic distribution. Oaxaca. Native speakers.","Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
1,Highland Oaxaca Chontal - WikipediaUC Berkeley Phonology Lab Annual Report (2005) THE PHONETIC ...Language - DOBES,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highland_Oaxaca_Chontal,"Tequistlatecan language is also referred to as the Chontal of Oaxaca. The distinct breakdown in the dialect of the Chontal of Oaxaca is as follows: Highland Chontal (the mountainous terrain) and Lowland Chontal (of Pacific coast). Abstract he first description in any detail of aspects of the phonetic structures of the highly endangered Lowland variety of Oaxaca Chontal i presented. The paper includes measurements of the characteristics of the vowels, and a survey of the principal features of the consonant system. Particular attention is paid to the series of glottali Enterprising second-language speakers have produced a DVD of traditional dances, a CD or two of popular music and a CD of children singing Chontal translations of Mexican patriotic songs.","Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
2,"Oaxaca Chontal (Tequistlateco, Tequistlatec, Huamelua, Sierra ...",https://www.native-languages.org/ochontal.htm,"Oaxacan Chontal has two dialects, Lowland (Huamelua) Chontal and Highland (Tequistlatec or Sierra) Chontal. Lowland and Highland Chontal are so distinct from one another than native speakers cannot understand each other well without a translator, and so most linguists consider them distinct languages.","Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
3,"Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca Language (CLO) – L1 & L2 Speakers ...",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/clo/,Lowland Oaxaca Chontal is an endangered indigenous language of Mexico. It belongs to the Tequistlatecan language family. The language is used as a first language by the elderly only.,"Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
4,"Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca language. Evangelism tools, church ...",https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/clo,"Short audio Bible stories and evangelistic messages that explain salvation and give basic Christian teaching. Each program is a customised and culturally relevant selection of scripts, and may include songs and music.","Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
5,"Tequistlatecan languages | Otomanguean, Mesoamerica... | Britannica",https://www.britannica.com/topic/Tequistlatecan-languages,Huamelultec (also called Lowland Chontal) is spoken today by fewer than 100 elderly persons in San Pedro Huamelula and Santiago Astata near the coast in the Mexican state of Oaxaca.,"Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
6,"Full text of ""enwiki-Highland_Oaxaca_Chontal-20200726.pdf""",https://archive.org/stream/enwiki-Highland_Oaxaca_Chontal-20200726.pdf/enwiki-Highland_Oaxaca_Chontal-20200726_djvu.txt,Highland Oaxaca Chontal (or Chontal de la Sierra de Oaxaca) is one of three Tequistlatecan languages family groups. The other two are Huamelultec (Lowland Oaxaca Chontal) and Tequistlatec (extinct).,"Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
7,Chontal from Oaxaca: Guardians of the Sierra Coastal Region,https://mexicanlibrary.org/indigenous-cultures-from-oaxaca-mexico-chontal-people/,"Language: Chontal of Oaxaca (Tequistlatecan family; two major varieties: Highland and Lowland). Main Symbols: The maize plant, wild agave mezcalero, the river-valley forest, and the sea turtle. Bioregion: Mixed montane and coastal ecosystems of the Sierra Madre del Sur and...","Chontal, Lowland Oaxaca",Tequistlatecan
